module core.frcp6

import core.calendar.{ district-state-holidays, us-federal-holidays }

/// FRCP 6(a) and 6(e) from the 1963 amendments (effective July 1, 1963):
/// Saturdays join Sundays and legal holidays as days a period cannot end
/// on, and inside a period of less than 7 days none of them is counted
/// (1963 Advisory Committee Note); service by mail adds 3 days, and the
/// other methods of former Rule 5(b), delivery and leaving a copy with the
/// clerk, none (the 2001 Committee Note: Rule 5(b)(2)(A)-(C) "carry forward
/// the method-of-service provisions of former Rule 5(b)").
///
/// Until December 1, 2005, Rule 6(e) said only that "3 days shall be
/// added to the prescribed period" (2005 Committee Note, text as amended:
/// sources/change-corpus/rules/committee-notes/note-civ-2005.txt). Whether
/// the days were added to the period before it was counted under 6(a), or
/// after it expired, was contested: the 2005 amendment was made "to remove
/// any doubt as to the method". The engine has one order, the 2005 one
/// (SPEC §5.3 step 5). UNVERIFIED for every pre-2005 version: no vendored
/// text or ruling fixes the earlier order, and the direction of risk is a
/// later due date, since for a period under the short-period threshold
/// the 2005 order gives more days than adding 3 to the period first.
///
/// Periods in months or years. Rule 6(a) reaches "any applicable statute"
/// and is silent on months and years; the courts count a year to the
/// anniversary of its start: "When a statute of limitations is measured in
/// years, the last day for instituting the action is the anniversary date
/// of the start of the limitations period. See, e.g., Fed.R.Civ.P. 6(a)"
/// (Ross v. Artuz, 150 F.3d 97, 103 (2d Cir. 1998);
/// sources/change-corpus/rulings/ross-2cir-1998.txt). A month runs to the
/// corresponding day of the later month. `end_of_month clamp` is the
/// earlier of the two readings of a month without that day (a February 29
/// anniversary); UNVERIFIED: no rule text or vendored decision fixes it.
/// No ruling in the corpus counts a year under this version.
counting frcp6 effective [1963-07-01, 1985-08-01) known_at 1963-01-21 cite "FRCP (2008 ed.) 6 (1963 Advisory Committee Note)" historical
{
  exclude_trigger_day
  include_last_day
  roll_forward_on [weekend, legal_holiday]
  short_period { below: 7.days, count: business_days }
  service_extension {
    Personal: 0.days
    Electronic: unrecognized
    Mail: 3.days
    Clerk: 0.days
    OtherConsented: unrecognized
    CommercialCarrier: unrecognized
    PrisonMail: unrecognized
    MailForeign: unrecognized
    Express: unrecognized
    ExpressForeign: unrecognized
  }
  holidays us-federal-holidays
  forums [frcp]
  end_of_month clamp
}

/// The 1985 amendments (effective August 1, 1985): the short-period
/// exclusion reaches periods of less than 11 days, and a last day on which
/// the clerk's office is inaccessible rolls forward too (1985 Advisory
/// Committee Note). Years count to the anniversary, as under the 1963
/// version: a one-year AEDPA period starting April 24, 1996 ends April 24,
/// 1997 under this text (Ross v. Artuz, 150 F.3d at 103; Flanagan v.
/// Johnson, 154 F.3d 196, 202 (5th Cir. 1998); Patterson v. Stewart, 251
/// F.3d 1243, 1246 (9th Cir. 2001)).
counting frcp6 effective [1985-08-01, 2001-12-01) known_at 1985-04-29 supersedes frcp6@1963-07-01 cite "FRCP (2008 ed.) 6 (1985 Advisory Committee Note)" historical
{
  exclude_trigger_day
  include_last_day
  roll_forward_on [weekend, legal_holiday, inaccessible]
  short_period { below: 11.days, count: business_days }
  service_extension {
    Personal: 0.days
    Electronic: unrecognized
    Mail: 3.days
    Clerk: 0.days
    OtherConsented: unrecognized
    CommercialCarrier: unrecognized
    PrisonMail: unrecognized
    MailForeign: unrecognized
    Express: unrecognized
    ExpressForeign: unrecognized
  }
  holidays us-federal-holidays
  forums [frcp]
  end_of_month clamp
}

/// The 2001 amendments (effective December 1, 2001): the 3 added days reach
/// service by leaving a copy with the clerk and by other consented means,
/// electronic service included (Rule 5(b)(2)(D) "including electronic
/// means", 532 U.S. 1089; 5(b)(2)(E) once restyled in 2007). Personal
/// delivery (5(b)(2)(A)) adds nothing in any version. The order of adding
/// them is the contested pre-2005 one (see the 1963 version).
/// Rule 6 is silent on periods stated in months or years (Rule 60(c)(1)'s
/// "a year"): they run to the corresponding day of the later month, the
/// anniversary for a year (Ross v. Artuz; see the 1963 version), and a
/// month without that day (a February 29 anniversary) ends on its last day.
/// `end_of_month clamp` is the earlier of the two readings of that day;
/// UNVERIFIED: no rule text or vendored decision fixes it.
counting frcp6 effective [2001-12-01, 2005-12-01) known_at 2001-04-23 supersedes frcp6@1985-08-01 cite "FRCP (2008 ed.) 6 (2001 Committee Note)" historical
{
  exclude_trigger_day
  include_last_day
  roll_forward_on [weekend, legal_holiday, inaccessible]
  short_period { below: 11.days, count: business_days }
  service_extension {
    Personal: 0.days
    Electronic: 3.days
    Mail: 3.days
    Clerk: 3.days
    OtherConsented: 3.days
    CommercialCarrier: unrecognized
    PrisonMail: unrecognized
    MailForeign: unrecognized
    Express: unrecognized
    ExpressForeign: unrecognized
  } cite "FRCP (2008 ed.) 6 (2001 Committee Note)"
  holidays us-federal-holidays
  forums [frcp]
  end_of_month clamp
}

/// The 2005 amendment (adopted April 25, 2005, effective December 1, 2005):
/// the 3 days "are added after the prescribed period would otherwise expire
/// under subdivision (a)", the order the pipeline uses (2005 Committee
/// Note); restyled in 2007 as 6(a)(2) and 6(d). The 2008 edition prints
/// the restyled text of December 1, 2007, changes the 2007 Committee Note
/// calls "stylistic only": a later text, cited for 2005-2007 on purpose.
counting frcp6 effective [2005-12-01, 2009-12-01) known_at 2005-04-25 supersedes frcp6@2001-12-01 cite "FRCP (2008 ed.) 6(a)(2)" historical
{
  exclude_trigger_day
  include_last_day
  roll_forward_on [weekend, legal_holiday, inaccessible]
  short_period { below: 11.days, count: business_days }
  service_extension {
    Personal: 0.days
    Electronic: 3.days
    Mail: 3.days
    Clerk: 3.days
    OtherConsented: 3.days
    CommercialCarrier: unrecognized
    PrisonMail: unrecognized
    MailForeign: unrecognized
    Express: unrecognized
    ExpressForeign: unrecognized
  } cite "FRCP (2008 ed.) 6(d)"
  holidays us-federal-holidays
  forums [frcp]
  end_of_month clamp
}

/// The 2009 time-computation amendments (adopted March 26, 2009, effective
/// December 1, 2009): every day counts (6(a)(1)(B)), periods may be stated
/// in hours (6(a)(2)), and the clerk's office inaccessibility rule is
/// 6(a)(3). 6(d) still adds 3 days after service "under Rule 5(b)(2)(C),
/// (D), (E), or (F)", electronic service (E) included (2011 ed.).
/// Rule 6 is silent on periods stated in months or years (Rule 60(c)(1)'s
/// "a year"): they run to the corresponding day of the later month, the
/// anniversary for a year (Ross v. Artuz; see the 1963 version), and a
/// month without that day (a February 29 anniversary) ends on its last day.
/// `end_of_month clamp` is the earlier of the two readings of that day;
/// UNVERIFIED: no rule text or vendored decision fixes it.
counting frcp6 effective [2009-12-01, 2016-12-01) known_at 2009-03-26 supersedes frcp6@2005-12-01 cite "FRCP (2011 ed.) 6(a)"
{
  exclude_trigger_day
  include_last_day
  roll_forward_on [weekend, legal_holiday, inaccessible]
  service_extension {
    Personal: 0.days
    Electronic: 3.days
    Mail: 3.days
    Clerk: 3.days
    OtherConsented: 3.days
    CommercialCarrier: unrecognized
    PrisonMail: unrecognized
    MailForeign: unrecognized
    Express: unrecognized
    ExpressForeign: unrecognized
  } cite "FRCP (2011 ed.) 6(d)"
  holidays us-federal-holidays
  forward_holidays district-state-holidays cite "FRCP 6(a)(6)(C)"
  forums [frcp]
  allow_hours
  end_of_month clamp
}

/// The 2016 amendment (adopted April 28, 2016, effective December 1, 2016)
/// removes electronic service from 6(d): the 3 days follow service "under
/// Rule 5(b)(2)(C) (mail), (D) (leaving with the clerk), or (F) (other means
/// consented to)" only (the amendment as printed in volume 578 of the U.S.
/// Reports; Committee Note, "to remove service by electronic means under
/// Rule 5(b)(2)(E) from the modes of service that allow 3 added days").
/// Electronic service adds nothing (2017 ed.).
counting frcp6 effective [2016-12-01, 2023-12-01) known_at 2016-04-28 supersedes frcp6@2009-12-01 cite "FRCP (2017 ed.) 6(a)"
{
  exclude_trigger_day
  include_last_day
  roll_forward_on [weekend, legal_holiday, inaccessible]
  service_extension {
    Personal: 0.days
    Electronic: 0.days
    Mail: 3.days
    Clerk: 3.days
    OtherConsented: 3.days
    CommercialCarrier: unrecognized
    PrisonMail: unrecognized
    MailForeign: unrecognized
    Express: unrecognized
    ExpressForeign: unrecognized
  } cite "FRCP (2017 ed.) 6(d)"
  holidays us-federal-holidays
  forward_holidays district-state-holidays cite "FRCP 6(a)(6)(C)"
  forums [frcp]
  allow_hours
  end_of_month clamp
}

/// The 2023 amendment (adopted April 24, 2023, effective December 1, 2023)
/// adds Juneteenth National Independence Day to 6(a)(6)(A)'s list. The
/// count is unchanged: `us-federal-holidays` has observed it since June 17,
/// 2021, when Congress declared it a holiday (6(a)(6)(B)).
counting frcp6 effective [2023-12-01, ..) known_at 2023-04-24 supersedes frcp6@2016-12-01 cite "FRCP 6(a)"
{
  exclude_trigger_day
  include_last_day
  roll_forward_on [weekend, legal_holiday, inaccessible]
  service_extension {
    Personal: 0.days
    Electronic: 0.days
    Mail: 3.days
    Clerk: 3.days
    OtherConsented: 3.days
    CommercialCarrier: unrecognized
    PrisonMail: unrecognized
    MailForeign: unrecognized
    Express: unrecognized
    ExpressForeign: unrecognized
  } cite "FRCP 6(d)"
  holidays us-federal-holidays
  forward_holidays district-state-holidays cite "FRCP 6(a)(6)(C)"
  forums [frcp]
  allow_hours
  end_of_month clamp
}

/// The court's determination under FRCP 86(a)(2)(B) that applying an
/// amendment in a pending action "would be infeasible or work an
/// injustice".
event PriorTimeRulesOrdered()

/// FRCP 86(a)(2): the 2009 amendments govern proceedings after December 1,
/// 2009 in actions then pending, unless the court determines otherwise
/// (the adopting order's "insofar as just and practicable").
transition frcp6-2009 amends frcp6@2009-12-01 cite "FRCP 86(a)(2)(B)"
{
  unless has_event(PriorTimeRulesOrdered()) -> frcp6@2005-12-01
  otherwise -> frcp6@2009-12-01
}

/// The court's determination under FRCP 86(a)(2)(B) that applying the
/// 2016 amendment of Rule 6(d) in a pending action "would be infeasible or
/// work an injustice", so electronic service keeps its 3 added days.
event PriorServiceRuleOrdered()

/// FRCP 86(a)(2): the 2016 amendments govern proceedings after December 1,
/// 2016 in actions then pending, unless the court determines otherwise.
/// The adopting order (April 28, 2016) says the same: they "shall govern in
/// all proceedings in civil cases thereafter commenced and, insofar as just
/// and practicable, all proceedings then pending" (the order as sent to
/// Congress, vendored under sources/change-corpus/rules/congress-packages/).
/// Its own event, so an order keeping the pre-2009 rules in a matter
/// pending since then does not also keep the 2009 text.
transition frcp6-2016 amends frcp6@2016-12-01 cite "FRCP 86(a)(2)(B)"
{
  unless has_event(PriorServiceRuleOrdered()) -> frcp6@2009-12-01
  otherwise -> frcp6@2016-12-01
}
